{
  "dom_id": "step-q16475",
  "name": "Question 4",
  "problem": "Which of the following is the graph of the polar curve $r=-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ?$",
  "worked_solution": "The polar equation $r=2a\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ defines a circle of radius $|a|$ centered at the point $(a,0).$\n\nIn our case, we have $r=-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,$ and therefore\n\n$$\na=\\frac{(-4)}{2}=-2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation $r=-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ gives a circle of radius $|-2|=2$ centered at the point $(-2,0),$ as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14225098/assets/946014c799034b7175481491be12ecf39315496de246bae366ef0ffa6ee7ec6c.png)",
